# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This lesson will introduce quantum noise and errors, including many of their sources, and drawing analogies with classical errors. Understanding the types of errors that can occur, and the ability to model noise using quantum channels, will be critical to understanding future lessons. Indeed, most methods of error handling require that we know something about the type of noise or errors we seek to reduce or correct.\n"
msgstr "Pelajaran ini akan memperkenalkan hingar dan ralat kuantum, termasuk pelbagai puncanya, dan membuat analogi dengan ralat klasik. Memahami jenis-jenis ralat yang boleh berlaku, serta keupayaan untuk memodelkan hingar menggunakan saluran kuantum, adalah penting untuk memahami pelajaran akan datang. Sesungguhnya, kebanyakan kaedah pengendalian ralat memerlukan kita mengetahui sesuatu tentang jenis hingar atau ralat yang ingin kita kurangkan atau betulkan.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This video gives an overview of noise and errors and calls out a few computational results. The text below adds more detail and explores some types of errors using Qiskit.\n"
msgstr "Video ini memberikan gambaran keseluruhan tentang hingar dan ralat serta menonjolkan beberapa hasil pengiraan. Teks di bawah menambah lebih banyak butiran dan meneroka beberapa jenis ralat menggunakan Qiskit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<IBMVideo id=\"135026311\" title=\"In this video, Chris Porter provides an overview of quantum noise and errors, their origin, how they accumulate, and how we can model them. The text below provides more detail.\"/>\n"
msgstr "<IBMVideo id=\"135026311\" title=\"Dalam video ini, Chris Porter memberikan gambaran keseluruhan tentang hingar dan ralat kuantum, asal-usulnya, cara ia terkumpul, dan cara kita boleh memodelkannya. Teks di bawah memberikan lebih banyak butiran.\"/>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Causes {#causes}"
msgstr "Punca {#causes}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Classical noise and errors {#classical-noise-and-errors}"
msgstr "Hingar dan ralat klasik {#classical-noise-and-errors}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "To understand quantum errors and noise, let’s start with the classical analog. Errors occur in classical computers for a variety of reasons, including multiple mechanisms for spurious charge accumulation, single-event upsets, and slow material degradation. Some of these mechanisms (not all) have analogs in quantum computing and will be instrumental in understanding quantum errors and noise. We briefly review a few of those and then move on to the quantum analogs.\n"
msgstr "Untuk memahami ralat dan hingar kuantum, mari kita mulakan dengan analog klasik. Ralat berlaku dalam komputer klasik atas pelbagai sebab, termasuk pelbagai mekanisme pengumpulan cas palsu, gangguan peristiwa tunggal, dan degradasi bahan yang perlahan. Sebahagian mekanisme ini (tidak semuanya) mempunyai analog dalam pengkomputeran kuantum dan akan penting dalam memahami ralat dan hingar kuantum. Kami akan mengulas secara ringkas beberapa daripadanya sebelum beralih kepada analog kuantum.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "__Thermal noise:__\n"
msgstr "__Hingar terma:__\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "As components of a computer heat up, the thermal motion of the atoms and charge carriers changes the resistivity of the material (usually increasing it). This means the resistance in the circuit can vary. Although rare, this can result in a spurious logic transition in a transistor. Although minor variations are inescapable, proper cooling of components is key in avoiding these effects.\n"
msgstr "Apabila komponen komputer menjadi panas, pergerakan terma atom dan pembawa cas mengubah kerintangan bahan (biasanya meningkatkannya). Ini bermakna rintangan dalam litar boleh berubah-ubah. Walaupun jarang berlaku, ini boleh mengakibatkan peralihan logik palsu dalam transistor. Walaupun variasi kecil tidak dapat dielakkan, penyejukan komponen yang betul adalah kunci untuk mengelakkan kesan ini.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "__Thermal damage and degradation:__\n"
msgstr "__Kerosakan dan degradasi terma:__\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Repeated heating and cooling of materials can cause very slow migration of conductive elements (metals). At best, this changes the resistance or capacitance (or both) of circuit elements and leads to imprecise timing in circuit operation. At worst, it results in a short-circuit or hard fault. Again, proper temperature control is key in preventing this type of degradation.\n"
msgstr "Pemanasan dan penyejukan bahan yang berulang boleh menyebabkan penghijrahan yang sangat perlahan bagi unsur konduktif (logam). Dalam keadaan terbaik, ini mengubah rintangan atau kapasitans (atau kedua-duanya) elemen litar dan membawa kepada ketepatan masa yang kurang tepat dalam operasi litar. Dalam keadaan terburuk, ia mengakibatkan litar pintas atau kerosakan keras. Sekali lagi, kawalan suhu yang betul adalah kunci untuk mencegah jenis degradasi ini.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "__Radiation-induced effects (single-event upsets, SEUs):__\n"
msgstr "__Kesan aruhan radiasi (gangguan peristiwa tunggal, SEU):__\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sometimes, high-energy particles can ionize atoms inside a processor and lead to a spurious charge. If it happens at the wrong place and wrong time, this can lead to logical errors. The source of these high-energy particles (including electromagnetic radiation and neutrons) is typically high-energy interactions in the upper atmosphere. These upsets are thus more common in high-altitude environments, including space. These are addressed using error-correcting codes and radiation-hard design.\n"
msgstr "Kadangkala, zarah bertenaga tinggi boleh mengionkan atom di dalam pemproses dan membawa kepada cas palsu. Jika ia berlaku pada tempat dan masa yang salah, ini boleh menyebabkan ralat logik. Punca zarah bertenaga tinggi ini (termasuk radiasi elektromagnet dan neutron) biasanya ialah interaksi bertenaga tinggi di atmosfera atas. Oleh itu, gangguan ini lebih kerap berlaku dalam persekitaran altitud tinggi, termasuk di angkasa. Ini ditangani menggunakan kod pembetulan ralat dan reka bentuk tahan radiasi.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "__Electromagnetic interference:__\n"
msgstr "__Gangguan elektromagnet:__\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Errors can also be caused by external electromagnetic (EM) fields from switching power lines, radio transmitters, or other system components. This is different from the high-energy radiation in the previous phenomenon. Here, stray time-dependent EM fields induce current and voltage across computing components (as opposed to ionizing a particular atom in one event). Because the time-dependence of fields is a determining factor in the induced voltages, this type of interference is especially important in high-frequency systems. These errors can be combatted through shielding and grounding, among other things.\n"
msgstr "Ralat juga boleh disebabkan oleh medan elektromagnet (EM) luaran daripada talian kuasa yang bertukar, pemancar radio, atau komponen sistem lain. Ini berbeza daripada radiasi bertenaga tinggi dalam fenomena sebelumnya. Di sini, medan EM sesat yang bergantung kepada masa mengaruhkan arus dan voltan merentasi komponen pengkomputeran (berbanding mengionkan satu atom tertentu dalam satu peristiwa). Oleh kerana kebergantungan masa medan adalah faktor penentu dalam voltan teraruh, jenis gangguan ini amat penting dalam sistem berfrekuensi tinggi. Ralat ini boleh ditangani melalui pelindungan dan pembumian, antara lain.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "__Crosstalk between interconnects:__\n"
msgstr "__Crosstalk antara sambungan:__\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Capacitive or inductive coupling between adjacent conductors, especially in nanoscale integrated circuits, can lead to spurious signals. This is related to electromagnetic interference, but it is different because the source of the offending field is another component inside the computer circuit. Think of two wires very close to each other. If wire A experiences a sudden transition (change in voltage) it can induce currents in the adjacent wire B. The exact direction and magnitude depend on circuit layout; it could be a coherent net flow of charge or something closer to eddy currents of charge. But in some cases this can lead to unwanted logical transitions. These crosstalk effects can be mitigated through optimal layout spacing, shielding, and controlled signal rise times.\n"
msgstr "Gandingan kapasitif atau induktif antara konduktor bersebelahan, terutamanya dalam litar bersepadu skala nano, boleh membawa kepada isyarat palsu. Ini berkaitan dengan gangguan elektromagnet, tetapi ia berbeza kerana punca medan yang menyebabkan gangguan itu ialah komponen lain di dalam litar komputer. Bayangkan dua wayar yang sangat berdekatan antara satu sama lain. Jika wayar A mengalami peralihan mendadak (perubahan voltan) ia boleh mengaruhkan arus dalam wayar bersebelahan B. Arah dan magnitud sebenar bergantung kepada susun atur litar; ia mungkin aliran cas bersih yang koheren atau sesuatu yang lebih menyerupai arus eddy cas. Tetapi dalam sesetengah kes ini boleh membawa kepada peralihan logik yang tidak diingini. Kesan crosstalk ini boleh dikurangkan melalui jarak susun atur yang optimum, pelindungan, dan masa naik isyarat yang terkawal.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This is not a complete list, but it is sufficient for us to begin to see parallels with quantum computing. Note that in all the types of unwanted interactions above, the manifestation of the noise as a logical error can only take the form of a 0 becoming a 1, or a 1 becoming a 0. This is all that is permitted in the state space of classical bits.\n"
msgstr "Ini bukanlah senarai yang lengkap, tetapi ia mencukupi untuk kita mula melihat persamaan dengan pengkomputeran kuantum. Perhatikan bahawa dalam semua jenis interaksi yang tidak diingini di atas, manifestasi hingar sebagai ralat logik hanya boleh berbentuk 0 bertukar menjadi 1, atau 1 bertukar menjadi 0. Inilah sahaja yang dibenarkan dalam ruang keadaan bit klasik.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Quantum noise and errors {#quantum-noise-and-errors}"
msgstr "Hingar dan ralat kuantum {#quantum-noise-and-errors}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In quantum computing, noise and errors are often discussed hand-in-hand, but it is important to recognize the difference. Quantum noise refers to the underlying physical disturbance affecting qubits and quantum systems. It includes all forms of unwanted interactions or fluctuations from the environment, imperfect controls, or hardware defects that cause the quantum state to drift away from the ideal. Quantum errors are the specific deviations or faults in the quantum information that result from the quantum noise. Quantum errors are the measurable or operational manifestations of the noise, and errors include bit-flips, phase-flips, amplitude damping, and more.\n"
"Both noise and errors can occur in a continuous way. For example, the field used in a quantum gate could be off by 0.1%, 0.15%, or any other fraction. This could result in a phase on a qubit that is incorrect, also by any continuous amount. But you will see that, in practice and especially in error correction, it is common to see errors treated as though they are discrete: grouping the accumulated effects into a discrete bit flip or a single phase rotation. This is useful for correcting errors and characterizing them, but it does not mean that the effect of noise on a qubit is solely a discrete set of errors.\n"
msgstr ""
"Dalam pengkomputeran kuantum, hingar dan ralat sering dibincangkan seiring, tetapi penting untuk mengenali perbezaannya. Hingar kuantum merujuk kepada gangguan fizikal asas yang menjejaskan qubit dan sistem kuantum. Ia merangkumi semua bentuk interaksi atau turun naik yang tidak diingini daripada persekitaran, kawalan yang tidak sempurna, atau kecacatan perkakasan yang menyebabkan keadaan kuantum tersasar daripada keadaan ideal. Ralat kuantum ialah sisihan atau kecacatan khusus dalam maklumat kuantum yang terhasil daripada hingar kuantum. Ralat kuantum ialah manifestasi hingar yang boleh diukur atau operasi, dan ralat termasuk bit-flip, phase-flip, pelemahan amplitud, dan lain-lain.\n"
"Kedua-dua hingar dan ralat boleh berlaku secara berterusan. Sebagai contoh, medan yang digunakan dalam gate kuantum boleh tersasar sebanyak 0.1%, 0.15%, atau sebarang pecahan lain. Ini boleh mengakibatkan fasa pada qubit yang tidak tepat, juga sebanyak sebarang jumlah berterusan. Tetapi anda akan lihat bahawa, dalam praktiknya dan terutamanya dalam pembetulan ralat, adalah lazim untuk melihat ralat dianggap seolah-olah ia diskret: mengumpulkan kesan terkumpul ke dalam satu bit flip diskret atau satu putaran fasa tunggal. Ini berguna untuk membetulkan ralat dan mencirikannya, tetapi ia tidak bermakna kesan hingar pada qubit hanyalah satu set ralat diskret.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "There are many types of noise that affect quantum computers. Some can result in multiple types of errors, while others are paired with specific error manifestations. A complete list is beyond the purposes of this lesson; here is a partial list relevant to the rest of this course, some with parallels to classical computing noise.\n"
msgstr "Terdapat banyak jenis hingar yang menjejaskan komputer kuantum. Sesetengahnya boleh mengakibatkan pelbagai jenis ralat, manakala yang lain dikaitkan dengan manifestasi ralat tertentu. Senarai yang lengkap adalah di luar skop pelajaran ini; berikut ialah senarai separa yang berkaitan dengan baki kursus ini, sesetengahnya mempunyai persamaan dengan hingar pengkomputeran klasik.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "__Thermal:__\n"
msgstr "__Thermal:__\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The two computational basis states $|0\\rangle$ and $|1\\rangle$ of a qubit are separated by a very small amount of energy. The exact value depends, of course, on the quantum hardware. For solid-state (fixed-frequency transmon) qubits such as those used by IBM Quantum&reg;, the energy gap between these states is on the order of $10^{-24}$ J, or a few microelectron volts. This energy scale can be related to the frequency via $E=hf$ or to a characteristic temperature. Since temperature is related to the average kinetic energy of particles in a sample, we can use the Boltzmann constant to obtain an estimate of the temperature at which average particles would have enough energy to cause a transition:\n"
msgstr "Dua keadaan asas pengiraan $|0\\rangle$ dan $|1\\rangle$ bagi satu qubit dipisahkan oleh jumlah tenaga yang sangat kecil. Nilai sebenar bergantung, sudah tentu, kepada perkakasan kuantum. Bagi qubit keadaan pepejal (transmon frekuensi tetap) seperti yang digunakan oleh IBM Quantum&reg;, jurang tenaga antara keadaan ini adalah pada tahap $10^{-24}$ J, atau beberapa mikroelektron volt. Skala tenaga ini boleh dikaitkan dengan frekuensi melalui $E=hf$ atau dengan suhu ciri. Oleh kerana suhu berkaitan dengan tenaga kinetik purata zarah dalam suatu sampel, kita boleh menggunakan pemalar Boltzmann untuk mendapatkan anggaran suhu di mana zarah purata mempunyai tenaga yang cukup untuk menyebabkan peralihan:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"E\\approx k_BT \\rightarrow T~E/k_B \\approx 10^{-24}\\text{ J } \\times 1.381 \\times 10^{−23}\\text{ J/K}\\approx 100 \\text{mK}.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"E\\approx k_BT \\rightarrow T~E/k_B \\approx 10^{-24}\\text{ J } \\times 1.381 \\times 10^{−23}\\text{ J/K}\\approx 100 \\text{mK}.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In order to reduce the chance that thermal interactions cause transitions from one qubit computational basis state to another, the temperature of the qubits must be maintained substantially below 100 mK, ideally closer to 15-25 mK. Any significant increase in temperature above these thresholds would result in totally random state occupation and total loss of quantum information.\n"
msgstr "Untuk mengurangkan kemungkinan interaksi terma menyebabkan peralihan daripada satu keadaan asas pengiraan qubit kepada yang lain, suhu qubit mesti dikekalkan jauh di bawah 100 mK, idealnya lebih hampir kepada 15-25 mK. Sebarang peningkatan suhu yang ketara melebihi ambang ini akan mengakibatkan penghunian keadaan yang benar-benar rawak dan kehilangan sepenuhnya maklumat kuantum.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This is analogous to the thermal errors that can occur in classical computers, in that they are both thermal in origin &mdash; but the temperature scale is obviously different, and so is the mechanism for information loss.\n"
msgstr "Ini adalah analog kepada ralat terma yang boleh berlaku dalam komputer klasik, kerana kedua-duanya berasal daripada punca terma &mdash; tetapi skala suhu jelas berbeza, begitu juga mekanisme kehilangan maklumat.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "__Crosstalk and interference:__\n"
msgstr "__Crosstalk dan gangguan:__\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Crosstalk in quantum computing refers to any unwanted coupling or interference between qubits or control lines such that an operation or signal intended for one qubit inadvertently affects others. It manifests as errors or correlations between qubits that should ideally be independent.\n"
msgstr "Crosstalk dalam pengkomputeran kuantum merujuk kepada sebarang gandingan atau gangguan yang tidak diingini antara qubit atau talian kawalan sedemikian rupa sehingga operasi atau isyarat yang dimaksudkan untuk satu qubit secara tidak sengaja menjejaskan qubit lain. Ia bermanifestasi sebagai ralat atau korelasi antara qubit yang sepatutnya bebas antara satu sama lain.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In superconducting qubit architectures like those used by IBM&reg;, crosstalk can arise from microwave control signals leaking into neighboring qubits, unintended coupling between readout resonators, or flux crosstalk between qubit loops.\n"
msgstr "Dalam senibina qubit superkonduktor seperti yang digunakan oleh IBM&reg;, crosstalk boleh timbul daripada isyarat kawalan gelombang mikro yang bocor ke qubit bersebelahan, gandingan yang tidak diingini antara resonator bacaan, atau crosstalk fluks antara gelung qubit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Many of the approaches to minimizing crosstalk are incorporated into the design of a quantum computer, such as spacing of qubits and ensuring adjacent qubits have different operational frequencies. There are also software approaches to mitigating crosstalk errors, which will be discussed in later lessons.\n"
msgstr "Banyak pendekatan untuk meminimumkan crosstalk digabungkan ke dalam reka bentuk komputer kuantum, seperti jarak antara qubit dan memastikan qubit bersebelahan mempunyai frekuensi operasi yang berbeza. Terdapat juga pendekatan perisian untuk mengurangkan ralat crosstalk, yang akan dibincangkan dalam pelajaran akan datang.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "__Imperfect gate operations:__\n"
msgstr "__Operasi gate yang tidak sempurna:__\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Operations on qubits are carried out using hardware that has finite precision and which can be subject to noise or other sources of inaccuracy. In particular, microwave pulses of specific durations and with specific orientations are used to implement rotation gates. Say for example that a microwave pulse is to rotate 0.30 radians around the $X$ axis. There is finite precision in the orientation, leading to some imprecision in the axis of rotation. Further, there is always a fundamental tradeoff between a pulse frequency and its duration. A very long pulse can have essentially a sinusoidal behavior with practically a single frequency. To form a short packet, one needs a superposition of many frequencies. This is the same tradeoff at the root of the energy-time uncertainty relation, although gate precision is not yet pushing up against these fundamental limits; there is room for improvement.\n"
msgstr "Operasi pada qubit dilaksanakan menggunakan perkakasan yang mempunyai ketepatan terhad dan boleh terdedah kepada hingar atau punca ketidaktepatan lain. Khususnya, denyut gelombang mikro dengan tempoh tertentu dan orientasi tertentu digunakan untuk melaksanakan gate putaran. Katakan sebagai contoh bahawa satu denyut gelombang mikro hendak memutar 0.30 radian mengelilingi paksi $X$. Terdapat ketepatan terhad dalam orientasi, yang membawa kepada sedikit ketidaktepatan dalam paksi putaran. Selain itu, sentiasa terdapat pertukaran asas antara frekuensi denyut dan tempohnya. Denyut yang sangat panjang boleh mempunyai kelakuan yang pada asasnya sinusoid dengan hampir satu frekuensi tunggal. Untuk membentuk paket yang pendek, seseorang memerlukan superposisi banyak frekuensi. Ini adalah pertukaran yang sama yang menjadi punca kepada hubungan ketidakpastian tenaga-masa, walaupun ketepatan gate belum lagi menghampiri had asas ini; masih ada ruang untuk penambahbaikan.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "__Quantum-inherent noise:__\n"
msgstr "__Hingar semula jadi kuantum:__\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This last type of noise is quite different from the others; it is not due to errors, but due to the nature of quantum mechanics. Suppose a qubit in quantum state $|\\psi\\rangle$ is measured and collapses to an eigenstate ($|0\\rangle$ or $|1\\rangle$). In a fixed-frequency transmon system, the qubit is dispersively coupled to a microwave resonator whose resonance frequency depends slightly on the qubit state. A microwave probe pulse is sent through the resonator, and the outgoing signal acquires a state-dependent phase shift and amplitude change. This signal is then amplified and measured using classical electronics.\n"
msgstr "Jenis hingar terakhir ini agak berbeza daripada yang lain; ia bukan disebabkan oleh ralat, tetapi disebabkan oleh sifat semula jadi mekanik kuantum. Andaikan satu qubit dalam keadaan kuantum $|\\psi\\rangle$ diukur dan runtuh kepada satu keadaan eigen ($|0\\rangle$ atau $|1\\rangle$). Dalam sistem transmon frekuensi tetap, qubit tersebut digandingkan secara dispersif kepada satu resonator gelombang mikro yang frekuensi resonannya sedikit bergantung kepada keadaan qubit. Satu denyut prob gelombang mikro dihantar melalui resonator tersebut, dan isyarat keluar memperoleh anjakan fasa dan perubahan amplitud yang bergantung kepada keadaan. Isyarat ini kemudian dikuatkan dan diukur menggunakan elektronik klasik.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "At the quantum level, the measurement signal is carried by a coherent microwave field with a finite average number of photons. Due to the quantum nature of electromagnetic fields, the field quadratures exhibit intrinsic fluctuations. These fluctuations are independent of technical imperfections and arise even for an ideal coherent state. Increasing the average photon number $N$ increases the measurement signal, but the fundamental noise level remains finite, so that the distinguishability between outcomes improves only as $\\sqrt{N}$​.\n"
msgstr "Pada peringkat kuantum, isyarat pengukuran dibawa oleh medan gelombang mikro koheren dengan bilangan foton purata yang terhad. Disebabkan sifat kuantum medan elektromagnet, kuadratur medan menunjukkan turun naik intrinsik. Turun naik ini tidak bergantung kepada ketidaksempurnaan teknikal dan timbul walaupun bagi keadaan koheren yang ideal. Meningkatkan bilangan foton purata $N$ meningkatkan isyarat pengukuran, tetapi tahap hingar asas kekal terhad, sehingga kebolehbezaan antara hasil hanya bertambah baik mengikut $\\sqrt{N}$​.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Thus, even if the qubit is perfectly prepared and the measurement chain is ideal, the readout signal corresponding to a given qubit state is not a single fixed value but a distribution that limits how well the outcomes can be distinguished. This overlap represents a fundamental source of uncertainty in the measurement process itself. In practice, while technical noise from amplifiers and other components often dominates, this intrinsic quantum noise sets a lower bound on readout fidelity.\n"
msgstr "Oleh itu, walaupun qubit disediakan dengan sempurna dan rantaian pengukuran adalah ideal, isyarat bacaan yang sepadan dengan keadaan qubit tertentu bukanlah satu nilai tetap tunggal tetapi satu taburan yang menghadkan sejauh mana hasil boleh dibezakan. Pertindihan ini mewakili satu punca ketidakpastian asas dalam proses pengukuran itu sendiri. Dalam praktiknya, walaupun hingar teknikal daripada penguat dan komponen lain sering mendominasi, hingar kuantum intrinsik ini menetapkan had bawah kepada kesetiaan bacaan.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "__Uncertainty is not error:__\n"
msgstr "__Ketidakpastian bukan ralat:__\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "These sources of noise are all different from the fundamental uncertainty associated with measurements of quantum states. For contrast, suppose that we have a qubit in a quantum state:\n"
msgstr "Semua punca hingar ini berbeza daripada ketidakpastian asas yang berkaitan dengan pengukuran keadaan kuantum. Sebagai perbandingan, andaikan kita mempunyai satu qubit dalam keadaan kuantum:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$$|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{4}{5}|0\\rangle+\\frac{3}{5}|1\\rangle.$$\n"
msgstr "$$|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{4}{5}|0\\rangle+\\frac{3}{5}|1\\rangle.$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This state has a 64% chance of being measured in the $|0\\rangle$ state and a 36% chance of being measured in the $|1\\rangle$ state. But a single measurement will only yield a $|0\\rangle$ or a $|1\\rangle$ &mdash; so a single measurement will rarely tell us much about the state prior to measurement. Many measurements are required to obtain detailed information about the state. Out of ten measurements, we might expect six or seven to yield $|0\\rangle$. But it would not be shocking if the first ten measurements all happened to yield $|0\\rangle$ (like flipping a fair coin and obtaining \"heads\" ten times in a row: uncommon, but entirely plausible). If we repeated the measurement 1000 times and always obtained $|0\\rangle$, that would indeed be shocking.\n"
msgstr "Keadaan ini mempunyai kebarangkalian 64% untuk diukur dalam keadaan $|0\\rangle$ dan kebarangkalian 36% untuk diukur dalam keadaan $|1\\rangle$. Tetapi satu pengukuran tunggal hanya akan menghasilkan $|0\\rangle$ atau $|1\\rangle$ &mdash; jadi satu pengukuran tunggal jarang memberitahu kita banyak tentang keadaan sebelum pengukuran. Banyak pengukuran diperlukan untuk mendapatkan maklumat terperinci tentang keadaan tersebut. Daripada sepuluh pengukuran, kita mungkin menjangkakan enam atau tujuh akan menghasilkan $|0\\rangle$. Tetapi ia tidak akan mengejutkan jika sepuluh pengukuran pertama semuanya kebetulan menghasilkan $|0\\rangle$ (seperti membaling syiling yang adil dan mendapat \"kepala\" sepuluh kali berturut-turut: jarang berlaku, tetapi sepenuhnya masuk akal). Jika kita mengulangi pengukuran 1000 kali dan sentiasa mendapat $|0\\rangle$, itu memang akan mengejutkan.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "There is therefore uncertainty in our knowledge of the quantum state based on any finite number of measurements. As we repeat the measurements, our knowledge of the quantum state prior to measurement improves, but it is never perfect. One might colloquially say that there is always some underlying \"error\" in any quantum measurement result. But this is a different use of the term \"error\" and this is better described as statistical uncertainty. The process of increasing knowledge of a quantum state through many measurements is called quantum state tomography. Quantum state tomography is not limited to measurements along Z, however; it generally refers to measurements along orthogonal axes (like X, Y, and Z) for determination of amplitudes and relative phase. For more on this process, see [this section](/learning/courses/general-formulation-of-quantum-information/general-measurements/discrimination-and-tomography) in John Watrous’s course on the [General Formulation of Quantum Information](/learning/courses/general-formulation-of-quantum-information), or for a practical implementation, see [this teleportation lesson](/learning/courses/utility-scale-quantum-computing/teleportation) of the course on [Utility-Scale Quantum Computing](/learning/courses/utility-scale-quantum-computing).\n"
msgstr "Oleh itu, terdapat ketidakpastian dalam pengetahuan kita tentang keadaan kuantum berdasarkan sebarang bilangan pengukuran yang terhad. Apabila kita mengulangi pengukuran, pengetahuan kita tentang keadaan kuantum sebelum pengukuran bertambah baik, tetapi ia tidak pernah sempurna. Seseorang mungkin secara santai mengatakan bahawa sentiasa terdapat sejenis \"ralat\" asas dalam sebarang hasil pengukuran kuantum. Tetapi ini adalah penggunaan istilah \"ralat\" yang berbeza dan ia lebih baik digambarkan sebagai ketidakpastian statistik. Proses meningkatkan pengetahuan tentang keadaan kuantum melalui banyak pengukuran dipanggil tomografi keadaan kuantum. Walau bagaimanapun, tomografi keadaan kuantum tidak terhad kepada pengukuran sepanjang Z sahaja; ia secara umumnya merujuk kepada pengukuran sepanjang paksi ortogon (seperti X, Y, dan Z) untuk penentuan amplitud dan fasa relatif. Untuk maklumat lanjut tentang proses ini, lihat [bahagian ini](/learning/courses/general-formulation-of-quantum-information/general-measurements/discrimination-and-tomography) dalam kursus John Watrous mengenai [Formulasi Umum Maklumat Kuantum](/learning/courses/general-formulation-of-quantum-information), atau untuk pelaksanaan praktikal, lihat [pelajaran teleportasi ini](/learning/courses/utility-scale-quantum-computing/teleportation) daripada kursus mengenai [Pengkomputeran Kuantum Skala Utiliti](/learning/courses/utility-scale-quantum-computing).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Observing errors and their accumulation {#observing-errors-and-their-accumulation}"
msgstr "Memerhati ralat dan pengumpulannya {#observing-errors-and-their-accumulation}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this section, we will use real quantum computers to observe quantum errors and their accumulation under different conditions. In practice, many sources of quantum errors are typically present simultaneously. In order to observe different types of errors in a somewhat isolated way, we will need to use circuits designed for that isolation. Circuits used in applications of quantum computing will look much more complicated.\n"
msgstr "Dalam bahagian ini, kita akan menggunakan komputer kuantum sebenar untuk memerhati ralat kuantum dan pengumpulannya di bawah keadaan yang berbeza. Dalam praktiknya, banyak punca ralat kuantum biasanya hadir secara serentak. Untuk memerhati jenis-jenis ralat yang berbeza secara agak terasing, kita perlu menggunakan litar yang direka khas untuk pengasingan itu. Litar yang digunakan dalam aplikasi pengkomputeran kuantum akan kelihatan jauh lebih rumit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let's start with energy relaxation errors.\n"
msgstr "Mari kita mulakan dengan ralat kelonggaran tenaga.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Energy relaxation ($T_1$) errors {#energy-relaxation-t-1-errors}"
msgstr "Ralat kelonggaran tenaga ($T_1$) {#energy-relaxation-t-1-errors}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Recall from our discussion of thermal noise that at any non-zero temperature, there is some probability of a quantum system undergoing a transition between energy eigenstates. The relaxation ($|1\\rangle \\rightarrow |0\\rangle$) dominates because it's driven by spontaneous emission (independent of temperature), while low temperature suppresses the competing excitation process. The longer a qubit sits idle, the more time it has to undergo such a transition. The probability that a qubit prepared in the state $|1\\rangle$ remains in $|1\\rangle$ after a time $t$ is given by\n"
msgstr "Ingat kembali dari perbincangan kita tentang hingar terma bahawa pada sebarang suhu bukan sifar, terdapat sedikit kebarangkalian bagi sistem kuantum mengalami peralihan antara keadaan eigen tenaga. Kelonggaran ($|1\\rangle \\rightarrow |0\\rangle$) mendominasi kerana ia didorong oleh pancaran spontan (tidak bergantung kepada suhu), manakala suhu rendah menindas proses pengujaan yang bersaing. Semakin lama qubit terbiar, semakin banyak masa yang ia ada untuk mengalami peralihan sedemikian. Kebarangkalian bahawa satu qubit yang disediakan dalam keadaan $|1\\rangle$ kekal dalam $|1\\rangle$ selepas masa $t$ diberikan oleh\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"p = e^{-t/T_1}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"p = e^{-t/T_1}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Here, the time $T_1$ is an experimentally-determined characteristic time that is different for every qubit, and may even change slightly over time. IBM&reg; quantum computers are benchmarked regularly, so the $T_1$ times that you find listed for qubits on IBM quantum computers should be accurate and up-to-date. A large value of $T_1$ means the qubit remains in the state you prepared longer (on average). Typical $T_1$ values differ across architectures; the qubits in IBM quantum computers often have $T_1$ times on the order of 200-300 $\\mu s$, and you can check the latest values on the [Compute resources](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/computers) page.\n"
msgstr "Di sini, masa $T_1$ ialah satu masa ciri yang ditentukan secara eksperimen yang berbeza bagi setiap qubit, dan mungkin berubah sedikit dari semasa ke semasa. Komputer kuantum IBM&reg; ditanda aras secara berkala, jadi masa $T_1$ yang anda dapati disenaraikan bagi qubit pada komputer kuantum IBM sepatutnya tepat dan terkini. Nilai $T_1$ yang besar bermakna qubit kekal dalam keadaan yang anda sediakan lebih lama (secara purata). Nilai $T_1$ tipikal berbeza mengikut senibina; qubit dalam komputer kuantum IBM selalunya mempunyai masa $T_1$ pada tahap 200-300 $\\mu s$, dan anda boleh menyemak nilai terkini pada halaman [Sumber pengkomputeran](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/computers).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The characteristic time $T_2$ measures something different: the loss of phase information. We will return to this later.\n"
msgstr "Masa ciri $T_2$ mengukur sesuatu yang berbeza: kehilangan maklumat fasa. Kita akan kembali kepada ini kemudian.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "To observe energy relaxation errors, we will prepare qubits in the $|1\\rangle$ state, and then intentionally leave them idle for long periods of time. This is the artificial part. In real quantum workflows, you might have a few qubits that sit idle for long periods while processes are carried out on other qubits; therefore, a few instances of such idle times is *not* artificial.\n"
msgstr "Untuk memerhati ralat kelonggaran tenaga, kita akan menyediakan qubit dalam keadaan $|1\\rangle$, kemudian dengan sengaja membiarkannya terbiar untuk tempoh masa yang panjang. Inilah bahagian yang tiruan. Dalam aliran kerja kuantum sebenar, anda mungkin mempunyai beberapa qubit yang terbiar untuk tempoh masa yang panjang semasa proses dijalankan pada qubit lain; oleh itu, beberapa contoh masa terbiar sedemikian *bukan*lah tiruan.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let's begin by importing some packages and selecting the least-busy backend.\n"
msgstr "Mari kita mulakan dengan mengimport beberapa pakej dan memilih backend yang paling kurang sibuk.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we will select a qubit to work on, in this case, qubit zero. We can directly import the properties of this qubit, including $T_1$ and $T_2$.\n"
msgstr "Sekarang kita akan memilih satu qubit untuk digunakan, dalam kes ini, qubit sifar. Kita boleh mengimport terus sifat-sifat qubit ini, termasuk $T_1$ dan $T_2$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Knowing $T_1$ in units of microseconds is important, but you might not have an intuitive feel for what that means. After all, gate operations on quantum computers are very fast. The real question is: how much can we do on a quantum computer in that amount of time. To this end, it is useful to relate $T_1$ to other time scales used in quantum computing &mdash; specifically to the following:\n"
msgstr "Mengetahui $T_1$ dalam unit mikrosaat adalah penting, tetapi anda mungkin tidak mempunyai gambaran intuitif tentang maksudnya. Bagaimanapun, operasi gate pada komputer kuantum adalah sangat pantas. Persoalan sebenar ialah: berapa banyak yang boleh kita lakukan pada komputer kuantum dalam jumlah masa tersebut. Untuk tujuan ini, adalah berguna untuk mengaitkan $T_1$ dengan skala masa lain yang digunakan dalam pengkomputeran kuantum &mdash; khususnya kepada yang berikut:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "sampling time $dt$ - the smallest clock tick over which control processes can be implemented on the quantum computer.\n"
msgstr "masa persampelan $dt$ - detik jam terkecil di mana proses kawalan boleh dilaksanakan pada komputer kuantum.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "single-qubit gate times - the time required to implement a gate operation on a single qubit. This will not be a single value, but a range of possible numbers depending on gate type.\n"
msgstr "masa gate satu-qubit - masa yang diperlukan untuk melaksanakan operasi gate pada satu qubit. Ini bukan satu nilai tunggal, tetapi satu julat nilai yang mungkin bergantung kepada jenis gate.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "two-qubit gate times - the time required to implement a gate operation on two adjacent qubits. This can also take on a range of values, but is typically about one order of magnitude longer than for a single-qubit gate.\n"
msgstr "masa gate dua-qubit - masa yang diperlukan untuk melaksanakan operasi gate pada dua qubit bersebelahan. Ini juga boleh mengambil julat nilai, tetapi biasanya kira-kira satu order magnitud lebih lama berbanding gate satu-qubit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let's check $dt$ for our backend, and display some plausible ranges for gate times.\n"
msgstr "Mari kita semak $dt$ untuk backend kita, dan paparkan beberapa julat munasabah untuk masa gate.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "These gate times will vary from backend to backend. We will hard-code a two-qubit gate time pulled from `ibm_boston` of 68 ns.\n"
msgstr "Masa gate ini akan berbeza-beza antara satu backend dengan yang lain. Kita akan mengkod-keras satu masa gate dua-qubit yang diambil daripada `ibm_boston` iaitu 68 ns.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Keep in mind that no other error sources nor their suppression or mitigation have been taken into account here. $T_1$ errors are not the only errors at work in a quantum system, so don't be too concerned about these numbers. This simply means, in the absence of any suppression, we might plausibly implement on the order of thousands of two-qubit gates before $T_1$ errors start to dominate. Other errors will typically dominate at much lower depths, and conversely $T_1$ errors might still be worth suppressing at much shorter times than those required to wash out the signal entirely.\n"
msgstr "Perlu diingat bahawa tiada punca ralat lain mahupun penindasan atau mitigasinya diambil kira di sini. Ralat $T_1$ bukanlah satu-satunya ralat yang bertindak dalam sistem kuantum, jadi jangan terlalu risau tentang angka-angka ini. Ini hanya bermakna, tanpa sebarang penindasan, kita mungkin secara munasabah boleh melaksanakan sehingga beribu-ribu gate dua-qubit sebelum ralat $T_1$ mula mendominasi. Ralat lain biasanya akan mendominasi pada kedalaman yang jauh lebih rendah, dan sebaliknya ralat $T_1$ mungkin masih berbaloi untuk ditindas pada masa yang jauh lebih singkat berbanding yang diperlukan untuk menghakis isyarat sepenuhnya.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We will start by using a simulator with a noise model, and then we will compare the results to real hardware.\n"
msgstr "Kita akan mulakan dengan menggunakan satu simulator dengan model hingar, kemudian kita akan membandingkan hasilnya dengan perkakasan sebenar.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let us define a time step for our measurements corresponding to the high-end estimate for a two-qubit gate time. Then we will build 50 circuits in increments of that time step. In this way, we will see what happens over a time scale similar to implementing 80 two-qubit gates. Precision here is not critical, so we will use only a handful of shots.\n"
msgstr "Mari kita takrifkan satu langkah masa untuk pengukuran kita yang sepadan dengan anggaran tinggi bagi masa gate dua-qubit. Kemudian kita akan membina 50 litar dalam pertambahan langkah masa tersebut. Dengan cara ini, kita akan melihat apa yang berlaku sepanjang skala masa yang serupa dengan melaksanakan 80 gate dua-qubit. Ketepatan di sini tidak kritikal, jadi kita hanya akan menggunakan segelintir shot.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We build a noise model for our simulator that explicitly includes thermal relaxation errors.\n"
msgstr "Kita membina satu model hingar untuk simulator kita yang secara eksplisit merangkumi ralat kelonggaran terma.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we build our circuits and observables. These are exceptionally simple. The circuits will consist simply of preparing the qubit in $|1\\rangle$ and implementing delays for increasing periods of time, using the characteristic gate times and $dt$ defined above. The observables will simply be $Z$, because what we want to know is whether the qubit has remained in $|1\\rangle$ or decayed to $|0\\rangle$. Although transpilation is not always necessary for simulators, we go ahead and transpile our circuits here for use later on a real backend.\n"
msgstr "Sekarang kita bina litar dan cerapan kita. Ini amat ringkas. Litar ini hanya terdiri daripada menyediakan qubit dalam $|1\\rangle$ dan melaksanakan kelewatan untuk tempoh masa yang semakin meningkat, menggunakan masa gate ciri dan $dt$ yang ditakrifkan di atas. Cerapan ini hanya akan menjadi $Z$, kerana apa yang kita ingin ketahui ialah sama ada qubit kekal dalam $|1\\rangle$ atau reput kepada $|0\\rangle$. Walaupun transpilasi tidak selalu diperlukan untuk simulator, kita tetap mentranspil litar kita di sini untuk kegunaan kemudian pada backend sebenar.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We run our job using a simulator.\n"
msgstr "Kita menjalankan tugasan kita menggunakan simulator.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Finally we plot the outputs.\n"
msgstr "Akhirnya kita plot output.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/noise-and-errors/extracted-outputs/2663fa60-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/noise-and-errors/extracted-outputs/2663fa60-0.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The exponential decay is clearly visible, and in this time, it decays to just under a 40% chance of being in $|1\\rangle$. Note that this is exactly consistent with the model: $p = e^{-t/t_1}$ where we have chosen the maximum $t$ to be on the same order as $t_1$; recall that we said $t_1$ might correspond to 60 gates (or that order of magnitude), and here $t$ corresponds to 60 gates. Thus\n"
"$$\n"
"p \\rightarrow e^{-0.25 \\text{ ms}/0.267\\text{ ms}} \\approx 0.392\n"
"$$\n"
"Now let's run the same circuits on a real quantum computer. In order to span the same delay duration with fewer jobs, we will increase the delay unit and decrease the number of points.\n"
msgstr ""
"Susutan eksponen jelas kelihatan, dan pada masa ini, ia susut kepada hanya di bawah 40% kebarangkalian untuk berada dalam $|1\\rangle$. Perhatikan bahawa ini adalah tepat konsisten dengan model: $p = e^{-t/t_1}$ di mana kita telah memilih $t$ maksimum berada pada tahap yang sama dengan $t_1$; ingat bahawa kita katakan $t_1$ mungkin sepadan dengan 60 gate (atau tahap magnitud itu), dan di sini $t$ sepadan dengan 60 gate. Oleh itu\n"
"$$\n"
"p \\rightarrow e^{-0.25 \\text{ ms}/0.267\\text{ ms}} \\approx 0.392\n"
"$$\n"
"Sekarang mari kita jalankan litar yang sama pada komputer kuantum sebenar. Untuk merangkumi tempoh kelewatan yang sama dengan lebih sedikit tugasan, kita akan meningkatkan unit kelewatan dan mengurangkan bilangan titik.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we plot the results.\n"
msgstr "Sekarang kita plot hasilnya.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/noise-and-errors/extracted-outputs/f6908468-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/noise-and-errors/extracted-outputs/f6908468-0.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As we can see, the decay of the $|1\\rangle$ state over time matches theory (and the definition of $T_1$). Further, the simulator is fairly good at matching the behavior of the real quantum computer in this case. Here are a few things to keep in mind about the decay of this state:\n"
"* We have artificially delayed measurement for an unrealistic amount of time. These delays correspond to the timescale of extremely deep circuits that could consist of thousands of two-qubit gates. This was done only to show that the effects of thermal noise exist. In real circuits applied to real-world problems, operations would be performed on other qubits while a handful might sit idle long enough for energy relaxation errors to matter &mdash; but other errors would dominate before reaching a full $T_1$.\n"
"* No error mitigation or suppression techniques have been used. We can alter this decay curve by using techniques we will learn in this course.\n"
"* We used only 100 shots here, which is unusually low because we sought only to demonstrate that the effect exists. You will likely use more than 1,000 shots in applications.\n"
msgstr ""
"Seperti yang kita lihat, susutan keadaan $|1\\rangle$ dari semasa ke semasa sepadan dengan teori (dan takrifan $T_1$). Selain itu, simulator agak baik dalam memadankan kelakuan komputer kuantum sebenar dalam kes ini. Berikut ialah beberapa perkara yang perlu diingat tentang susutan keadaan ini:\n"
"* Kita telah secara tiruan melewatkan pengukuran untuk satu jumlah masa yang tidak realistik. Kelewatan ini sepadan dengan skala masa litar yang amat dalam yang boleh terdiri daripada beribu-ribu gate dua-qubit. Ini dilakukan hanya untuk menunjukkan bahawa kesan hingar terma wujud. Dalam litar sebenar yang digunakan untuk masalah dunia sebenar, operasi akan dilaksanakan pada qubit lain manakala segelintir mungkin terbiar cukup lama untuk ralat kelonggaran tenaga menjadi penting &mdash; tetapi ralat lain akan mendominasi sebelum mencapai $T_1$ yang penuh.\n"
"* Tiada teknik mitigasi atau penindasan ralat digunakan. Kita boleh mengubah lengkung susutan ini dengan menggunakan teknik yang akan kita pelajari dalam kursus ini.\n"
"* Kita hanya menggunakan 100 shot di sini, yang luar biasa rendah kerana kita hanya ingin menunjukkan bahawa kesan ini wujud. Anda berkemungkinan akan menggunakan lebih daripada 1,000 shot dalam aplikasi.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This first visualization of error required almost no gates other than delays (and the single X gate used to prepare $|1\\rangle$). Let us now look at another example that focuses on gate error.\n"
msgstr "Visualisasi ralat pertama ini hampir tidak memerlukan sebarang gate selain kelewatan (dan satu gate X tunggal yang digunakan untuk menyediakan $|1\\rangle$). Mari kita lihat sekarang satu contoh lain yang memberi tumpuan kepada ralat gate.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Gate errors and accumulation {#gate-errors-and-accumulation}"
msgstr "Ralat gate dan pengumpulan {#gate-errors-and-accumulation}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"When we apply a quantum gate, say an $X$ gate, theoretically that gate rotates a quantum state by angle $\\pi$ radians about the $X$ axis on the Bloch sphere, mapping $\\alpha |0\\rangle + \\beta |1\\rangle$ to $\\alpha |1\\rangle + \\beta |0\\rangle$ via\n"
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"X|\\psi\\rangle & = R_X(\\pi) |\\psi\\rangle\\\\\n"
"& = \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos{(\\theta/2)} & -i \\sin{(\\theta/2)} \\\\ -i \\sin{(\\theta/2)} & \\cos{(\\theta/2)} \\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix} \\alpha \\\\ \\beta \\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"& = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix} \\alpha \\\\ \\beta \\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"& =\\begin{pmatrix} \\beta \\\\ \\alpha \\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
"However, a real gate is imperfect. It might be imperfectly oriented (causing rotations not perfectly about the X axis) or it might cause a slightly greater or smaller rotation than intended. Let us model a gate error as being purely over/under rotation by a small angle $\\epsilon$.\n"
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"X_\\epsilon|\\psi\\rangle & = R_X(\\pi+\\epsilon) |\\psi\\rangle\\\\\n"
"& = \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos{(\\theta+\\epsilon/2)} & -i \\sin{(\\theta+\\epsilon/2)} \\\\ -i \\sin{(\\theta+\\epsilon/2)} & \\cos{(\\theta+\\epsilon/2)} \\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix} \\alpha \\\\ \\beta \\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"& \\approx \\begin{pmatrix} \\epsilon & 1-\\epsilon^2/2 \\\\ 1-\\epsilon^2/2 & \\epsilon \\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix} \\alpha \\\\ \\beta \\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"& =\\begin{pmatrix} \\epsilon \\alpha+(1-\\epsilon^2/2)\\beta \\\\ (1-\\epsilon^2/2)\\alpha+\\epsilon \\beta \\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
"If an imperfect gate is applied many times, small errors $\\epsilon$ can compound. Sometimes these multiple errors even cause the state to shift in the same direction each time (a phenomenon called \"coherent accumulation\", which we will revisit below). Let's see what happens when we apply a real X gate to a qubit many times.\n"
msgstr ""
"Apabila kita mengaplikasikan satu quantum gate, katakan satu gate $X$, secara teorinya gate itu memutarkan satu keadaan kuantum sebanyak sudut $\\pi$ radian mengelilingi paksi $X$ pada sfera Bloch, memetakan $\\alpha |0\\rangle + \\beta |1\\rangle$ kepada $\\alpha |1\\rangle + \\beta |0\\rangle$ melalui\n"
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"X|\\psi\\rangle & = R_X(\\pi) |\\psi\\rangle\\\\\n"
"& = \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos{(\\theta/2)} & -i \\sin{(\\theta/2)} \\\\ -i \\sin{(\\theta/2)} & \\cos{(\\theta/2)} \\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix} \\alpha \\\\ \\beta \\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"& = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix} \\alpha \\\\ \\beta \\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"& =\\begin{pmatrix} \\beta \\\\ \\alpha \\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
"Walau bagaimanapun, gate sebenar tidak sempurna. Ia mungkin berorientasi tidak sempurna (menyebabkan putaran yang tidak tepat mengelilingi paksi X) atau ia mungkin menyebabkan putaran yang sedikit lebih besar atau lebih kecil daripada yang dimaksudkan. Mari kita model ralat gate sebagai putaran lebih/kurang secara tulen sebanyak sudut kecil $\\epsilon$.\n"
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"X_\\epsilon|\\psi\\rangle & = R_X(\\pi+\\epsilon) |\\psi\\rangle\\\\\n"
"& = \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos{(\\theta+\\epsilon/2)} & -i \\sin{(\\theta+\\epsilon/2)} \\\\ -i \\sin{(\\theta+\\epsilon/2)} & \\cos{(\\theta+\\epsilon/2)} \\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix} \\alpha \\\\ \\beta \\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"& \\approx \\begin{pmatrix} \\epsilon & 1-\\epsilon^2/2 \\\\ 1-\\epsilon^2/2 & \\epsilon \\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix} \\alpha \\\\ \\beta \\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"& =\\begin{pmatrix} \\epsilon \\alpha+(1-\\epsilon^2/2)\\beta \\\\ (1-\\epsilon^2/2)\\alpha+\\epsilon \\beta \\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
"Jika satu gate yang tidak sempurna diaplikasikan berkali-kali, ralat kecil $\\epsilon$ boleh berkumpul. Kadangkala ralat berbilang ini malah menyebabkan keadaan beralih ke arah yang sama setiap kali (satu fenomena yang dipanggil \"pengumpulan koheren\", yang akan kita bincang semula di bawah). Mari kita lihat apa yang berlaku apabila kita mengaplikasikan satu gate X sebenar kepada satu qubit berkali-kali.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/noise-and-errors/extracted-outputs/b2e02bc6-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/noise-and-errors/extracted-outputs/b2e02bc6-0.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The expectation value starts out around 1.0 as expected, and decays, but it doesn't just decay to zero! It becomes negative. If this were thermal noise or random interactions, it would not make $|1\\rangle$ more likely to be measured than $|0\\rangle$. This error is clearly different. And it makes sense in the context of our over/under-rotation discussion. If each rotation pair that should cancel actually ends up rotating by a small angle, then many such repetitions would slowly allow the state to rotate from $|0\\rangle$ toward $|1\\rangle$.\n"
msgstr "Nilai jangkaan bermula sekitar 1.0 seperti yang dijangka, dan mereput, tetapi ia bukan sekadar mereput kepada sifar! Ia menjadi negatif. Jika ini adalah hingar terma atau interaksi rawak, ia tidak akan menyebabkan $|1\\rangle$ lebih berkemungkinan diukur berbanding $|0\\rangle$. Ralat ini jelas berbeza. Dan ini masuk akal dalam konteks perbincangan lebih/kurang putaran kita. Jika setiap pasangan putaran yang sepatutnya saling membatalkan sebenarnya berakhir dengan berputar sebanyak sudut kecil, maka banyak pengulangan sebegini akan perlahan-lahan membenarkan keadaan berputar daripada $|0\\rangle$ ke arah $|1\\rangle$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The accumulation of errors shown here is not likely to proceed as cleanly in a real workflow. For one thing, we have explicitly turned off error mitigation and performed the same repetitive gate sequence to allow errors to accumulate coherently. This is done solely to demonstrate that these errors exist. When we use noise-handling techniques (for example, Pauli twirling), we can influence how error accumulates, often yielding lower overall error.\n"
msgstr "Pengumpulan ralat yang ditunjukkan di sini berkemungkinan tidak berlaku dengan sebersih ini dalam aliran kerja sebenar. Sebagai satu perkara, kita telah mematikan mitigasi ralat secara eksplisit dan melaksanakan jujukan gate berulang yang sama untuk membolehkan ralat berkumpul secara koheren. Ini dilakukan semata-mata untuk menunjukkan bahawa ralat ini wujud. Apabila kita menggunakan teknik pengendalian hingar (contohnya, Pauli twirling), kita boleh mempengaruhi cara ralat berkumpul, selalunya menghasilkan jumlah ralat yang lebih rendah.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We can check exactly how well our model of this error fits the experimental data by building a noise model explicitly and using a simulator. In the model below, we explicitly build in an error corresponding to 0.025 radians. That is not a magic number; one can simply tune the error until it reasonably describes the experiment.\n"
msgstr "Kita boleh menyemak dengan tepat sejauh mana model ralat ini sepadan dengan data eksperimen dengan membina model hingar secara eksplisit dan menggunakan simulator. Dalam model di bawah, kita secara eksplisit membina ralat yang bersamaan dengan 0.025 radian. Itu bukan nombor ajaib; seseorang boleh sekadar menala ralat itu sehingga ia menggambarkan eksperimen dengan munasabah.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we can plot the real and simulated curves together.\n"
msgstr "Sekarang kita boleh memplot lengkung sebenar dan lengkung simulasi bersama-sama.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/noise-and-errors/extracted-outputs/bbc3a2ca-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/noise-and-errors/extracted-outputs/bbc3a2ca-0.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The error in the real circuit was fairly well described by the over/under-rotation error model. Clearly different kinds of noise and errors will accumulate differently and result in drastically different effects on the fidelity of our quantum circuits. It thus becomes very important to classify and characterize the errors that can occur more formally than we have thus far.\n"
msgstr "Ralat dalam litar sebenar digambarkan dengan agak baik oleh model ralat lebih/kurang putaran. Jelas sekali jenis hingar dan ralat yang berbeza akan berkumpul secara berbeza dan menghasilkan kesan yang sangat berbeza terhadap fideliti litar kuantum kita. Oleh itu, menjadi sangat penting untuk mengklasifikasikan dan mencirikan ralat yang boleh berlaku secara lebih formal daripada yang telah kita lakukan setakat ini.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Characterizing quantum errors {#characterizing-quantum-errors}"
msgstr "Mencirikan ralat kuantum {#characterizing-quantum-errors}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We have already described some of the sources of quantum noise, and we could categorize errors according to the categories of noise that cause them. But there could be different categories of quantum errors that are useful in error correction and mitigation. For example, we need to know at what point errors occur and how errors accumulate if we want to correct or mitigate them. In specifying where (in what processes) errors occur, a common important category is state preparation and measurement errors (or SPAM errors, for short). Here, the term “state preparation” does not mean everything that is done on a state preparing it for measurement. Rather, it refers to initialization of the qubits, the “preparation” of the qubits for the execution of an algorithm, and so this SPAM category excludes things like gate errors during algorithm execution.\n"
msgstr "Kita telah pun menerangkan sebahagian daripada punca hingar kuantum, dan kita boleh mengkategorikan ralat mengikut kategori hingar yang menyebabkannya. Tetapi mungkin terdapat kategori ralat kuantum yang berbeza yang berguna dalam pembetulan dan mitigasi ralat. Sebagai contoh, kita perlu tahu pada titik mana ralat berlaku dan bagaimana ralat berkumpul jika kita mahu membetulkan atau mengurangkannya. Dalam menentukan di mana (dalam proses apa) ralat berlaku, satu kategori penting yang biasa ialah ralat penyediaan keadaan dan pengukuran (atau ralat SPAM, secara ringkas). Di sini, istilah \"penyediaan keadaan\" tidak bermaksud segala yang dilakukan pada satu keadaan untuk menyediakannya bagi pengukuran. Sebaliknya, ia merujuk kepada permulaan qubit, \"penyediaan\" qubit untuk pelaksanaan satu algoritma, dan justeru kategori SPAM ini tidak termasuk perkara seperti ralat gate semasa pelaksanaan algoritma.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Another important distinction is whether the errors are coherent or incoherent. To summarize briefly (we discuss them more in detail below), coherent errors are typically modeled by unitary operations and preserve state purity. Incoherent errors are modeled by non-unitary quantum channels and generally reduce state purity. It’s easy to visualize incoherent errors as thermal interactions that cause loss of quantum information, but really they could be caused by any random coupling to the environment. For an example of a coherent error, suppose there is an imperfection in pulse orientation in implementing an RY gate such that an $RY(\\theta)$ gate actually causes $RY(\\theta) \\cdot RX(\\epsilon)$. Such an imperfection would always cause an erroneous rotation around X in the same direction. This would be a coherent error.\n"
msgstr "Satu lagi perbezaan penting ialah sama ada ralat itu koheren atau tidak koheren. Untuk meringkaskan secara ringkas (kita akan membincangkannya dengan lebih terperinci di bawah), ralat koheren biasanya dimodelkan oleh operasi unitari dan mengekalkan ketulenan keadaan. Ralat tidak koheren dimodelkan oleh saluran kuantum bukan unitari dan secara amnya mengurangkan ketulenan keadaan. Mudah untuk membayangkan ralat tidak koheren sebagai interaksi terma yang menyebabkan kehilangan maklumat kuantum, tetapi sebenarnya ia boleh disebabkan oleh sebarang gandingan rawak dengan persekitaran. Sebagai contoh ralat koheren, andaikan terdapat ketidaksempurnaan dalam orientasi denyut semasa melaksanakan satu gate RY sehingga satu gate $RY(\\theta)$ sebenarnya menyebabkan $RY(\\theta) \\cdot RX(\\epsilon)$. Ketidaksempurnaan sebegini akan sentiasa menyebabkan putaran salah di sekeliling X dalam arah yang sama. Ini akan menjadi satu ralat koheren.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Note that SPAM errors include a mix of coherent errors, like systematic control faults, and incoherent errors, such as photon loss and thermal effects. Because this involves multiple steps and error sources, SPAM errors are best described as a combination of both coherent and incoherent errors.\n"
msgstr "Perhatikan bahawa ralat SPAM merangkumi gabungan ralat koheren, seperti kerosakan kawalan sistematik, dan ralat tidak koheren, seperti kehilangan foton dan kesan terma. Kerana ini melibatkan pelbagai langkah dan punca ralat, ralat SPAM paling baik digambarkan sebagai gabungan kedua-dua ralat koheren dan tidak koheren.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "SPAM errors {#spam-errors}"
msgstr "Ralat SPAM {#spam-errors}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As the name suggests, SPAM errors arise at two distinct stages of a quantum experiment: initial preparation and readout/measurement.\n"
"State preparation errors occur when the hardware fails to reliably initialize qubits in the desired starting state ($|0\\rangle$ for IBM quantum computers). Causes include:\n"
"* thermal excitation (real qubits are not perfectly cooled and can occasionally start in $|1\\rangle$ instead of $|0\\rangle$)\n"
"* residual photons or noise in resonators in superconducting qubits\n"
"* drifts in calibration that affect how accurately qubits are reset\n"
msgstr ""
"Seperti yang disarankan oleh namanya, ralat SPAM timbul pada dua peringkat berbeza dalam satu eksperimen kuantum: penyediaan awal dan bacaan/pengukuran.\n"
"Ralat penyediaan keadaan berlaku apabila perkakasan gagal memulakan qubit dengan boleh dipercayai pada keadaan permulaan yang dikehendaki ($|0\\rangle$ untuk komputer kuantum IBM). Punca termasuk:\n"
"* pengujaan terma (qubit sebenar tidak disejukkan dengan sempurna dan kadangkala boleh bermula dalam $|1\\rangle$ dan bukannya $|0\\rangle$)\n"
"* foton baki atau hingar dalam resonator dalam qubit superkonduktor\n"
"* hanyutan dalam penentukuran yang menjejaskan ketepatan penetapan semula qubit\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Measurement errors occur during the readout process, when the measurement apparatus misidentifies the qubit state.\n"
"Sources include:\n"
msgstr ""
"Ralat pengukuran berlaku semasa proses bacaan, apabila peralatan pengukuran salah mengenal pasti keadaan qubit.\n"
"Punca termasuk:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "readout amplifier noise causing confusion between $|0\\rangle$ and $|1\\rangle$\n"
msgstr "hingar penguat bacaan yang menyebabkan kekeliruan antara $|0\\rangle$ dan $|1\\rangle$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "qubit relaxation during readout (for example, a qubit in $|1\\rangle$ decays to $|0\\rangle$ just before or during measurement)\n"
msgstr "kelonggaran qubit semasa bacaan (contohnya, satu qubit dalam $|1\\rangle$ mereput kepada $|0\\rangle$ sejurus sebelum atau semasa pengukuran)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "crosstalk between readout lines: reading one qubit slightly influences readings on neighboring qubits\n"
msgstr "gangguan silang antara talian bacaan: membaca satu qubit sedikit mempengaruhi bacaan pada qubit bersebelahan\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Imperfect discrimination thresholds in the IBM resonator-based measurement systems\n"
msgstr "Ambang diskriminasi yang tidak sempurna dalam sistem pengukuran berasaskan resonator IBM\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "SPAM errors are primarily incoherent. Preparation errors act like random mixtures of excited and ground states. Measurement errors behave like classical confusion: with probability $p$, $|0\\rangle$ is reported as $|1\\rangle$ or vice versa. These are distinct from coherent unitary errors (like miscalibrated gates causing systematic phase shifts). SPAM errors instead resemble random classical noise, though rare coherent biases in readout can exist.\n"
msgstr "Ralat SPAM secara utamanya tidak koheren. Ralat penyediaan bertindak seperti campuran rawak keadaan teruja dan keadaan dasar. Ralat pengukuran bertindak seperti kekeliruan klasik: dengan kebarangkalian $p$, $|0\\rangle$ dilaporkan sebagai $|1\\rangle$ atau sebaliknya. Ini berbeza daripada ralat unitari koheren (seperti gate yang ditentukur secara salah menyebabkan anjakan fasa sistematik). Sebaliknya, ralat SPAM menyerupai hingar klasik rawak, walaupun bias koheren yang jarang berlaku dalam bacaan boleh wujud.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "SPAM errors can be problematic for randomized benchmarking or for short-depth circuits where state preparation and measurement represent a large fraction of total error. They are less of an issue for very deep circuits, especially those with many two-qubit gates.\n"
msgstr "Ralat SPAM boleh menjadi masalah untuk penanda aras rawak atau untuk litar kedalaman pendek di mana penyediaan keadaan dan pengukuran mewakili sebahagian besar daripada jumlah ralat. Ia kurang menjadi isu untuk litar yang sangat dalam, terutamanya yang mempunyai banyak gate dua-qubit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Mathematically, SPAM errors can be described as producing a noisy distribution $\\vec{p}_\\text{noisy}$ that is related to the ideal distribution $\\vec{p}_\\text{ideal}$ through a matrix $A$:\n"
msgstr "Secara matematik, ralat SPAM boleh digambarkan sebagai menghasilkan satu taburan hingar $\\vec{p}_\\text{noisy}$ yang berkait dengan taburan unggul $\\vec{p}_\\text{ideal}$ melalui satu matriks $A$:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\vec{p}_\\text{noisy} = A \\vec{p}_\\text{ideal}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\vec{p}_\\text{noisy} = A \\vec{p}_\\text{ideal}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Here $p_i$ is the probability of measuring bitstring $i$, and $A_{ij}$ is the probability of obtaining state $j$ when the state should have been prepared in state $i$. Picture the context of sampled bitstrings from Sampler. Your measured distribution of states will consist of many accurate measurements, but also some measurements in which instructions for state preparation do not match the observed state. This means the probabilities will be slightly off.\n"
msgstr "Di sini $p_i$ ialah kebarangkalian mengukur bitstring $i$, dan $A_{ij}$ ialah kebarangkalian memperoleh keadaan $j$ apabila keadaan itu sepatutnya telah disediakan dalam keadaan $i$. Bayangkan konteks bitstring yang disampel daripada Sampler. Taburan keadaan yang anda ukur akan terdiri daripada banyak pengukuran yang tepat, tetapi juga sebahagian pengukuran di mana arahan untuk penyediaan keadaan tidak sepadan dengan keadaan yang diperhatikan. Ini bermakna kebarangkalian akan sedikit terpesong.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![A two-part figure. On the left is a histogram showing the ideal and noisy measurement results. On the right is a reduced assignment matrix mapping probabilities from the noisy measured results to the ideal case.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/noise-and-errors/spam-errors.avif)\n"
msgstr "![A two-part figure. On the left is a histogram showing the ideal and noisy measurement results. On the right is a reduced assignment matrix mapping probabilities from the noisy measured results to the ideal case.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/noise-and-errors/spam-errors.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In the figure above, part (a) shows the ideal distribution of a GHZ state in teal, and the actual measurements in purple. In part (b) we see an example of an $A$ matrix. Note that the matrix is not perfectly symmetric across the diagonal, since each qubit need not be equally likely to erroneously return a 0 and to erroneously return a 1.\n"
"In practice, it is often hard to separate preparation and readout errors, so they are often combined in a single treatment (hence the common use of SPAM).\n"
msgstr ""
"Dalam rajah di atas, bahagian (a) menunjukkan taburan unggul bagi satu keadaan GHZ dalam warna teal, dan pengukuran sebenar dalam warna ungu. Dalam bahagian (b) kita lihat satu contoh matriks $A$. Perhatikan bahawa matriks itu tidak simetri secara sempurna merentasi pepenjuru, kerana setiap qubit tidak semestinya sama berkemungkinan untuk secara salah memulangkan 0 dan secara salah memulangkan 1.\n"
"Dalam praktiknya, selalunya sukar untuk memisahkan ralat penyediaan dan bacaan, jadi ia selalunya digabungkan dalam satu rawatan tunggal (justeru penggunaan lazim SPAM).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "One way of correcting these SPAM errors is by using simple circuits with known ideal measurement outcomes and comparing those to the measured outcomes in the presence of SPAM errors. This can be used to reverse-engineer the ideal outcomes of more complex circuits. For general high-entropy states, this could require constructing a matrix with $2^N\\times2^N$ probabilities, but in less extreme cases these errors can be mitigated.\n"
msgstr "Satu cara untuk membetulkan ralat SPAM ini ialah dengan menggunakan litar ringkas dengan hasil pengukuran unggul yang diketahui dan membandingkannya dengan hasil yang diukur apabila terdapat ralat SPAM. Ini boleh digunakan untuk merekayasa balik hasil unggul bagi litar yang lebih kompleks. Bagi keadaan entropi tinggi secara am, ini mungkin memerlukan pembinaan satu matriks dengan $2^N\\times2^N$ kebarangkalian, tetapi dalam kes yang kurang ekstrem ralat ini boleh dikurangkan.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Coherent errors versus incoherent errors {#coherent-errors-versus-incoherent-errors}"
msgstr "Ralat koheren berbanding ralat tidak koheren {#coherent-errors-versus-incoherent-errors}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "__Coherent errors__ are systematic, deterministic, and unitary mis-rotations of the quantum state. They arise from imperfect quantum operations that deviate in a predictable way. Examples include slight errors in rotation applied by single-qubit rotation gates, including those caused by calibration drift which would consistently over- or under-rotate. Mathematically, we could write that a real rotation gate, say $\\tilde{R_X}(\\theta)$ is given by the ideal rotation gate plus some additional error:\n"
msgstr "__Ralat koheren__ ialah putaran-salah yang sistematik, deterministik, dan unitari bagi keadaan kuantum. Ia timbul daripada operasi kuantum yang tidak sempurna yang menyimpang dengan cara yang boleh diramal. Contoh termasuk ralat kecil dalam putaran yang diaplikasikan oleh gate putaran satu-qubit, termasuk yang disebabkan oleh hanyutan penentukuran yang secara konsisten akan berputar lebih atau kurang. Secara matematik, kita boleh menulis bahawa satu gate putaran sebenar, katakan $\\tilde{R_X}(\\theta)$ diberikan oleh gate putaran unggul ditambah beberapa ralat tambahan:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\tilde{R_X}(\\theta) = R_X(\\theta + \\epsilon)\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\tilde{R_X}(\\theta) = R_X(\\theta + \\epsilon)\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "for some small under- or over-rotation $\\epsilon$. Other examples include a two-qubit gate adding a small unwanted phase shift and control pulses that systematically deviate from the ideal shape.\n"
msgstr "bagi sesuatu putaran kurang atau lebih yang kecil $\\epsilon$. Contoh lain termasuk satu gate dua-qubit yang menambah anjakan fasa kecil yang tidak diingini dan denyut kawalan yang secara sistematik menyimpang daripada bentuk unggul.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A key characteristic of coherent errors is that they are deterministic and can be modeled by unitary gates, themselves. Correspondingly they preserve the purity of the quantum state: the quantum information is not lost, it has simply been processed imperfectly.\n"
"Another important point to note is that repeated coherent errors interfere constructively and sometimes grow quadratically with the number of gates.\n"
msgstr ""
"Satu ciri utama ralat koheren ialah ia deterministik dan boleh dimodelkan oleh gate unitari itu sendiri. Selaras dengan itu ia mengekalkan ketulenan keadaan kuantum: maklumat kuantum tidak hilang, ia hanya diproses secara tidak sempurna.\n"
"Satu lagi perkara penting yang perlu diperhatikan ialah ralat koheren yang berulang saling mengganggu secara membina dan kadangkala berkembang secara kuadratik dengan bilangan gate.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "These errors are sometimes predictable and can thus be calibrated away to some extent. But the coherent errors that remain may accumulate quickly, causing troublesome deviations from ideal states. This means that sometimes it is advantageous to intentionally \"randomize\" coherent errors. This procedure and its usefulness will be covered in the lesson on error mitigation.\n"
msgstr "Ralat ini kadangkala boleh diramal dan justeru boleh ditentukur sehingga hilang setakat tertentu. Tetapi ralat koheren yang tertinggal mungkin berkumpul dengan cepat, menyebabkan penyimpangan yang menyusahkan daripada keadaan unggul. Ini bermakna kadangkala ia bermanfaat untuk secara sengaja \"merawakkan\" ralat koheren. Prosedur ini dan kegunaannya akan dibincangkan dalam pelajaran mengenai mitigasi ralat.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "Why they are troublesome {#why-they-are-troublesome}"
msgstr "Mengapa ia menyusahkan {#why-they-are-troublesome}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Coherent errors can “pile up” in dangerous ways, producing large deviations from the correct state if not randomized or corrected.\n"
msgstr "Ralat koheren boleh \"bertimbun\" dengan cara yang berbahaya, menghasilkan penyimpangan besar daripada keadaan yang betul jika tidak dirawakkan atau dibetulkan.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "__Incoherent errors__ are stochastic, random, and often non-unitary. They arise from interactions with the environment or uncontrolled noise sources. We have already seen some examples: energy relaxation / $T_1$ decay is incoherent. So are stochastic gate noise, thermal noise, and measurement error.\n"
msgstr "__Ralat tidak koheren__ adalah stokastik, rawak, dan selalunya bukan unitari. Ia timbul daripada interaksi dengan persekitaran atau punca hingar yang tidak terkawal. Kita telah pun melihat beberapa contoh: kelonggaran tenaga / peluruhan $T_1$ adalah tidak koheren. Begitu juga dengan hingar gate stokastik, hingar terma, dan ralat pengukuran.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The key feature of incoherent errors is that they are not the same each time an operation is performed. They are non-unitary, so they cannot be modeled by a simple unitary quantum gate as coherent errors could. Because they are incoherent, they typically accumulate in a linear way like random noise, not like a consistent over-rotation.\n"
msgstr "Ciri utama ralat tidak koheren ialah ia tidak sama setiap kali satu operasi dilaksanakan. Ia bukan unitari, jadi ia tidak boleh dimodelkan oleh satu gate kuantum unitari mudah sebagaimana ralat koheren boleh. Kerana ia tidak koheren, ia biasanya berkumpul secara linear seperti hingar rawak, bukan seperti putaran-lebih yang konsisten.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Incoherent errors cause qubits to lose information irreversibly (loss of purity and phase information).\n"
msgstr "Ralat tidak koheren menyebabkan qubit kehilangan maklumat secara tidak boleh diterbalikkan (kehilangan ketulenan dan maklumat fasa).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "So which is worse? As with most things in quantum computing, it depends. The takeaway is that coherent errors accumulate faster and non-linearly (which is bad). But they can be partially calibrated away and the phase information is still there in your system, if you can figure out how to properly extract it. Incoherent errors accumulate linearly, which is good for two reasons: it is slower than coherent accumulation and it is fairly easy to see how effects of incoherent errors scale for processes like zero-noise extrapolation (ZNE), which will be covered in the lesson on error mitigation. However, when incoherent errors occur, quantum information is truly lost, not merely obscured.\n"
msgstr "Jadi yang mana lebih teruk? Seperti kebanyakan perkara dalam pengkomputeran kuantum, ia bergantung. Intipatinya ialah ralat koheren berkumpul lebih cepat dan secara tidak linear (yang mana buruk). Tetapi ia boleh ditentukur sebahagiannya sehingga hilang dan maklumat fasa masih ada dalam sistem anda, jika anda dapat mencari cara untuk mengekstraknya dengan betul. Ralat tidak koheren berkumpul secara linear, yang mana baik atas dua sebab: ia lebih perlahan berbanding pengumpulan koheren dan agak mudah untuk melihat bagaimana kesan ralat tidak koheren berskala bagi proses seperti ekstrapolasi sifar-hingar (ZNE), yang akan dibincangkan dalam pelajaran mengenai mitigasi ralat. Walau bagaimanapun, apabila ralat tidak koheren berlaku, maklumat kuantum benar-benar hilang, bukan sekadar tersembunyi.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Density matrices and quantum channels {#density-matrices-and-quantum-channels}"
msgstr "Matriks ketumpatan dan saluran kuantum {#density-matrices-and-quantum-channels}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the following sections, we will go deeper into a few types of coherent and incoherent errors and see how they can be modeled mathematically using quantum channels. First, a brief refresher on density matrices and quantum channels is in order. For a full, rigorous treatment of these, see John Watrous's course [General formulation of quantum information](/learning/courses/general-formulation-of-quantum-information) on IBM Quantum Learning. The treatment here will be example-driven and less formal.\n"
msgstr "Dalam bahagian berikut, kita akan menyelami dengan lebih mendalam beberapa jenis ralat koheren dan tidak koheren dan melihat bagaimana ia boleh dimodelkan secara matematik menggunakan saluran kuantum. Pertama, satu imbasan ringkas mengenai matriks ketumpatan dan saluran kuantum adalah wajar. Untuk rawatan yang lengkap dan teliti mengenai ini, lihat kursus John Watrous [General formulation of quantum information](/learning/courses/general-formulation-of-quantum-information) di IBM Quantum Learning. Rawatan di sini akan didorong oleh contoh dan kurang formal.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"__Quantum channels__ are the most general way to describe how a quantum system evolves, interacts, and undergoes noise.\n"
"If you’re used to pure states and unitary evolution, quantum channels are the natural next step.\n"
msgstr ""
"__Saluran kuantum__ ialah cara paling umum untuk menggambarkan bagaimana satu sistem kuantum berevolusi, berinteraksi, dan mengalami hingar.\n"
"Jika anda biasa dengan keadaan tulen dan evolusi unitari, saluran kuantum adalah langkah seterusnya yang semula jadi.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In standard quantum mechanics, a \"pure\" state is written as $|\\psi\\rangle$. Here \"pure\" refers to purity of information or knowledge of preparation of the state; it does *not* mean an eigenstate of some system. For a qubit example, $|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)$ is a pure state because we have perfect knowledge of the relative amplitudes and phase.\n"
"The time evolution of such a pure state is governed by a unitary operator $U$:\n"
msgstr ""
"Dalam mekanik kuantum piawai, satu keadaan \"tulen\" ditulis sebagai $|\\psi\\rangle$. Di sini \"tulen\" merujuk kepada ketulenan maklumat atau pengetahuan tentang penyediaan keadaan itu; ia *bukan* bermaksud satu eigenkeadaan bagi sesuatu sistem. Sebagai contoh qubit, $|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)$ ialah satu keadaan tulen kerana kita mempunyai pengetahuan yang sempurna tentang amplitud dan fasa relatif.\n"
"Evolusi masa bagi keadaan tulen sebegini dikawal oleh satu operator unitari $U$:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\psi\\rangle \\rightarrow U|\\psi\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"|\\psi\\rangle \\rightarrow U|\\psi\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "as per the Schr\\\"odinger equation, in which $U = e^{-iHt/\\hbar}$, where $H$ is the system Hamiltonian and $\\hbar$ is the reduced Planck's constant, often \"set equal to one\" or more rigorously, absorbed into the Hamiltonian so that all energies are given in units of $\\hbar$.\n"
msgstr "berdasarkan persamaan Schr\\\"odinger, di mana $U = e^{-iHt/\\hbar}$, dengan $H$ ialah Hamiltonian sistem dan $\\hbar$ ialah pemalar Planck terturun, selalunya \"ditetapkan sama dengan satu\" atau secara lebih teliti, diserap ke dalam Hamiltonian supaya semua tenaga diberikan dalam unit $\\hbar$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Unitary evolution is reversible, deterministic, and isolated. But this is inadequate to describe real quantum systems. In real quantum systems:\n"
"* We perform measurements, collapsing the quantum state into an eigenstate of the system. This is explicitly non-unitary.\n"
"* The system interacts with an environment. This means the system is not truly isolated, and since the environment cannot be tracked ad infinitum it corresponds to loss of information.\n"
"* Practically speaking: noise corrupts the state.\n"
"And more.\n"
msgstr ""
"Evolusi unitari adalah boleh diterbalikkan, deterministik, dan terasing. Tetapi ini tidak mencukupi untuk menggambarkan sistem kuantum sebenar. Dalam sistem kuantum sebenar:\n"
"* Kita melakukan pengukuran, meruntuhkan keadaan kuantum kepada satu eigenkeadaan sistem. Ini secara eksplisit bukan unitari.\n"
"* Sistem berinteraksi dengan persekitaran. Ini bermakna sistem tidak benar-benar terasing, dan kerana persekitaran tidak boleh dijejaki ad infinitum ia bersamaan dengan kehilangan maklumat.\n"
"* Secara praktikalnya: hingar merosakkan keadaan.\n"
"Dan banyak lagi.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unitary evolution alone cannot describe these processes; therefore, we need a more general representation of quantum states and processes. A mixed state describes a *classical distribution* of quantum states. That is, we are already familiar with the fact that the quantum state: $|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)$ has a 50% chance of being *measured* (collapsed into classical information) in the state $|0\\rangle$ and a 50% chance of being measured in $|1\\rangle$. But the claim is that the initial quantum state is exactly $|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)$ with 100% probability. Now we want to add on top of the quantum mechanical probability a classical probability, for example:\n"
msgstr "Evolusi unitari sahaja tidak dapat menggambarkan proses-proses ini; oleh itu, kita memerlukan satu perwakilan yang lebih umum bagi keadaan dan proses kuantum. Satu keadaan bercampur menggambarkan satu *taburan klasik* bagi keadaan kuantum. Iaitu, kita sudah biasa dengan fakta bahawa keadaan kuantum: $|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)$ mempunyai peluang 50% untuk *diukur* (runtuh kepada maklumat klasik) dalam keadaan $|0\\rangle$ dan peluang 50% untuk diukur dalam $|1\\rangle$. Tetapi dakwaannya ialah keadaan kuantum awal adalah tepat $|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)$ dengan kebarangkalian 100%. Sekarang kita ingin menambah di atas kebarangkalian mekanik kuantum itu satu kebarangkalian klasik, sebagai contoh:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The initial quantum state is prepared in $|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)$ with a 25% classical probability, and is prepared in $|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle-i|1\\rangle)$ with a 75% classical probability (note the different relative phases).\n"
msgstr "Keadaan kuantum awal disediakan dalam $|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)$ dengan kebarangkalian klasik 25%, dan disediakan dalam $|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle-i|1\\rangle)$ dengan kebarangkalian klasik 75% (perhatikan fasa relatif yang berbeza).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sometimes this can be due to explicit classical probabilities in preparation; other times it can be due to entanglement with parts of the environment that we ignore. Either way, the need is clear for a tool to describe this classical distribution of quantum mechanical states. Pure states alone cannot represent these possibilities. Therefore, we introduce the __density matrix__.\n"
msgstr "Kadangkala ini boleh disebabkan oleh kebarangkalian klasik yang eksplisit dalam penyediaan; pada masa lain ia boleh disebabkan oleh keterjeratan dengan bahagian persekitaran yang kita abaikan. Walau apa pun, keperluan adalah jelas untuk satu alat bagi menggambarkan taburan klasik keadaan mekanik kuantum ini. Keadaan tulen sahaja tidak dapat mewakili kemungkinan-kemungkinan ini. Oleh itu, kita memperkenalkan __matriks ketumpatan__.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A mixed state is described by a density operator:\n"
msgstr "Satu keadaan bercampur digambarkan oleh satu operator ketumpatan:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\rho = \\sum_i p_i |\\psi_i\\rangle\\langle\\psi_i|.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\rho = \\sum_i p_i |\\psi_i\\rangle\\langle\\psi_i|.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Here, $|\\psi_i\\rangle$ is a quantum state (not necessarily an eigenstate of any particular operator) and $p_i$ is the classical probability that the system is prepared in the state $|\\psi_i\\rangle$. This is a good prescription for how to build a density matrix from a known classical distribution of quantum states. On the other hand, we might want to know given both a classical distribution of quantum states and a quantum mechanical probability of different measurement outcomes, what is the probability that a measurement yields a particular state (like $|0\\rangle$, or $|1\\rangle$, or other states when more qubits are involved). That is where the density matrix is especially useful. We can also write it in a basis $\\{|i\\rangle\\}$ as:\n"
msgstr "Di sini, $|\\psi_i\\rangle$ ialah satu keadaan kuantum (tidak semestinya satu eigenkeadaan bagi mana-mana operator tertentu) dan $p_i$ ialah kebarangkalian klasik bahawa sistem disediakan dalam keadaan $|\\psi_i\\rangle$. Ini adalah satu preskripsi yang baik tentang cara membina satu matriks ketumpatan daripada taburan klasik keadaan kuantum yang diketahui. Sebaliknya, kita mungkin ingin tahu, memandangkan kedua-dua taburan klasik keadaan kuantum dan kebarangkalian mekanik kuantum bagi hasil pengukuran yang berbeza, apakah kebarangkalian bahawa satu pengukuran menghasilkan satu keadaan tertentu (seperti $|0\\rangle$, atau $|1\\rangle$, atau keadaan lain apabila lebih banyak qubit terlibat). Itulah di mana matriks ketumpatan amat berguna. Kita juga boleh menulisnya dalam satu asas $\\{|i\\rangle\\}$ sebagai:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\rho = \\sum_{i,j} \\rho_{ij} |i\\rangle\\langle j|.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\rho = \\sum_{i,j} \\rho_{ij} |i\\rangle\\langle j|.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Here the diagonal elements $\\rho_{ii}$​ are probabilities:\n"
msgstr "Di sini unsur pepenjuru $\\rho_{ii}$​ adalah kebarangkalian:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "$\\rho_{00}$​ = probability the qubit is in $|0\\rangle$\n"
msgstr "$\\rho_{00}$​ = kebarangkalian qubit berada dalam $|0\\rangle$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "$\\rho_{11}$​ = probability the qubit is in $|1\\rangle$\n"
msgstr "$\\rho_{11}$​ = kebarangkalian qubit berada dalam $|1\\rangle$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "They are real, non‑negative, and sum to 1.\n"
msgstr "Ia adalah nyata, bukan‑negatif, dan berjumlah 1.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Off-diagonal elements $\\rho_{ij}, i \\neq j$ are coherences: phase relationships or interference capability, and generally correspond to how much quantum mechanical information is in the state. This is an informal statement, but let's see what it means in practice.\n"
msgstr "Unsur bukan-pepenjuru $\\rho_{ij}, i \\neq j$ adalah koherens: hubungan fasa atau keupayaan interferens, dan secara amnya bersamaan dengan berapa banyak maklumat mekanik kuantum yang ada dalam keadaan itu. Ini adalah satu kenyataan tidak formal, tetapi mari kita lihat apa maksudnya dalam praktik.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "Example {#example}"
msgstr "Contoh {#example}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Consider the pure state $|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)$.\n"
"(a) What do you know about probabilities of measurement and phase information without doing any math?\n"
"(b) Build the density matrix and verify your intuition.\n"
msgstr ""
"Pertimbangkan keadaan tulen $|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)$.\n"
"(a) Apakah yang anda tahu tentang kebarangkalian pengukuran dan maklumat fasa tanpa melakukan sebarang pengiraan matematik?\n"
"(b) Bina matriks ketumpatan dan sahkan intuisi anda.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Solution:\n"
msgstr "Solution:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(a) Because it is a pure state, there is only one possible phase between $|0\\rangle$ and $|1\\rangle$: +1. This is perfect phase information, so we expect off-diagonal elements in our density matrix to be large. You might have more intuition than that about the off-diagonals, but that is sufficient for now. Further, the amplitudes of the two basis states are equal, so we expect a 50% probability of measuring either $|0\\rangle$ or $|1\\rangle$. So we expect the diagonal entries to be 1/2.\n"
"(b) Using the definition above:\n"
"$$\n"
"\\rho = \\sum_i p_i |\\psi_i\\rangle\\langle\\psi_i|.\n"
"$$\n"
"$p_i \\neq 0$ only for the state $|\\psi\\rangle$. So we can write\n"
msgstr ""
"(a) Kerana ia adalah satu keadaan tulen, hanya terdapat satu fasa yang mungkin antara $|0\\rangle$ dan $|1\\rangle$: +1. Ini adalah maklumat fasa yang sempurna, jadi kita menjangkakan unsur bukan-pepenjuru dalam matriks ketumpatan kita adalah besar. Anda mungkin mempunyai lebih intuisi berbanding itu tentang unsur bukan-pepenjuru, tetapi itu memadai buat masa ini. Selanjutnya, amplitud kedua-dua keadaan asas adalah sama, jadi kita menjangkakan kebarangkalian 50% untuk mengukur sama ada $|0\\rangle$ atau $|1\\rangle$. Jadi kita menjangkakan entri pepenjuru adalah 1/2.\n"
"(b) Menggunakan takrifan di atas:\n"
"$$\n"
"\\rho = \\sum_i p_i |\\psi_i\\rangle\\langle\\psi_i|.\n"
"$$\n"
"$p_i \\neq 0$ hanya untuk keadaan $|\\psi\\rangle$. Jadi kita boleh menulis\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\rho & = 1 |\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi| \\\\\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)(\\langle0|+\\langle1|)\\\\\n"
"& =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{pmatrix}.\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\rho & = 1 |\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi| \\\\\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)(\\langle0|+\\langle1|)\\\\\n"
"& =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{pmatrix}.\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The diagonal entries are each $1/2$ as expected, and the off-diagonal elements are large (sum to one).\n"
msgstr "Entri pepenjuru masing-masing adalah $1/2$ seperti yang dijangka, dan unsur bukan-pepenjuru adalah besar (berjumlah satu).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now let's see what happens when we do not have good phase information.\n"
msgstr "Sekarang mari kita lihat apa yang berlaku apabila kita tidak mempunyai maklumat fasa yang baik.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "Example {#example-1}"
msgstr "Contoh {#example-1}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Consider a similar case, but now with a 50% classical probability of being in the state $|\\psi_+\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)$ and a 50% classical probability of being in the state $|\\psi_-\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle-|1\\rangle)$. Same two questions:\n"
"(a) What do you know about probabilities of measurement and phase information without doing any math?\n"
"(b) Build the density matrix and verify your intuition.\n"
msgstr ""
"Pertimbangkan satu kes yang serupa, tetapi kini dengan kebarangkalian klasik 50% berada dalam keadaan $|\\psi_+\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)$ dan kebarangkalian klasik 50% berada dalam keadaan $|\\psi_-\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle-|1\\rangle)$. Soalan yang sama:\n"
"(a) Apakah yang anda tahu tentang kebarangkalian pengukuran dan maklumat fasa tanpa melakukan sebarang pengiraan matematik?\n"
"(b) Bina matriks ketumpatan dan sahkan intuisi anda.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(a) As before, the amplitudes of the two basis states are equal, so we expect a 50% probability of measuring either $|0\\rangle$ or $|1\\rangle$. So we expect the diagonal entries to be 1/2. But this time there are two opposite possibilities for the phase between $|0\\rangle$ and $|1\\rangle$: +1 and -1, and each one is equally likely. One might expect that with two equally likely opposite phases, one has little to no phase information. One might expect the off-diagonal entries to be small.\n"
"(b) Again using the definition above\n"
"$$\n"
"\\rho = \\sum_i p_i |\\psi_i\\rangle\\langle\\psi_i|.\n"
"$$\n"
"$p_i \\neq 0$ only for the two states $|\\psi_+\\rangle$ and $|\\psi_-\\rangle$. So we can write\n"
msgstr ""
"(a) Seperti sebelum ini, amplitud kedua-dua keadaan asas adalah sama, jadi kita menjangkakan kebarangkalian 50% untuk mengukur sama ada $|0\\rangle$ atau $|1\\rangle$. Jadi kita menjangkakan entri pepenjuru adalah 1/2. Tetapi kali ini terdapat dua kemungkinan bertentangan untuk fasa antara $|0\\rangle$ dan $|1\\rangle$: +1 dan -1, dan setiap satu sama berkemungkinan. Seseorang mungkin menjangkakan bahawa dengan dua fasa bertentangan yang sama berkemungkinan, seseorang mempunyai sedikit atau tiada maklumat fasa. Seseorang mungkin menjangkakan entri bukan-pepenjuru adalah kecil.\n"
"(b) Sekali lagi menggunakan takrifan di atas\n"
"$$\n"
"\\rho = \\sum_i p_i |\\psi_i\\rangle\\langle\\psi_i|.\n"
"$$\n"
"$p_i \\neq 0$ hanya untuk kedua-dua keadaan $|\\psi_+\\rangle$ dan $|\\psi_-\\rangle$. Jadi kita boleh menulis\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\rho & = \\frac{1}{2} |\\psi_+\\rangle\\langle\\psi_+| + \\frac{1}{2}|\\psi_-\\rangle\\langle\\psi_-| \\\\\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{4}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)(\\langle0|+\\langle1|)+\\frac{1}{4}(|0\\rangle-|1\\rangle)(\\langle0|-\\langle1|)\\\\\n"
"& =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{pmatrix}+\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"& =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{pmatrix}.\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\rho & = \\frac{1}{2} |\\psi_+\\rangle\\langle\\psi_+| + \\frac{1}{2}|\\psi_-\\rangle\\langle\\psi_-| \\\\\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{4}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)(\\langle0|+\\langle1|)+\\frac{1}{4}(|0\\rangle-|1\\rangle)(\\langle0|-\\langle1|)\\\\\n"
"& =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{pmatrix}+\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n"
"& =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{pmatrix}.\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The off-diagonal entries are small, zero in fact, as expected. The diagonal elements are still 50% each, which makes sense because each of the states in the mixture had $|0\\rangle$ and $|1\\rangle$ equally weighted.\n"
msgstr "Entri bukan-pepenjuru adalah kecil, sifar malah, seperti yang dijangka. Unsur pepenjuru masih 50% masing-masing, yang masuk akal kerana setiap keadaan dalam campuran itu mempunyai $|0\\rangle$ dan $|1\\rangle$ diberi berat yang sama.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-09-26
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This construct becomes more interesting as the number of qubits increases. Explore this in the questions below.\n"
msgstr "Konstruk ini menjadi lebih menarik apabila bilangan qubit meningkat. Terokai ini dalam soalan-soalan di bawah.\n"

